From: EglantinePS5 <Vampinity@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: up: page range walk-ahead, kept unbroken GPU memory runs across page table changes (EDEN_NX2_PAGE_RANGE_WALK_AHEAD)

Patch 0582 (the draft's own number was 0581; renumbered at placement, 0581 is lean SSBO).
Port of waveydude/Eglantine-PS5- 4af9c45353 ("Wave Ocarina 01", 11 Nov 2026 20:35Z), his core
patch 0920 "Reuse GPU page-table walks across nearby buffer bindings", credit waveydude for the
adaptation to our tree. The design is heydemoura's: EglantinePS5 PR #100 (commit 32433a9ec,
merged as cec2d7e03), his core patch 0076-page-range-cache-walk-ahead. His switch name is kept.
His code is taken line for line; this port adds only the counters, the boot line and a verify
switch (marked "port addition" in the code).

What: each storage buffer bind asks MaxContinuousRange "how far from this GPU address is memory
one unbroken block?" (a walk over the GPU page table, up to 8 MiB a time, ~220,000 times a second
in Ocarina). Ours keeps exact (address, size) answers per thread (0055/0062) but forgets all of
them at every page table change. With the switch, each change logs its address range (the last
64 changes), and a missed question walks up to 256 MiB ahead; the unbroken run it finds answers
every later question that starts inside it (16 runs per thread), and a run is dropped only when
a logged change touches it (ends included).

Switches (boot, read once; exact "1", anything else off; off by default):
  EDEN_NX2_PAGE_RANGE_WALK_AHEAD=1         the above (his).
  EDEN_NX2_PAGE_RANGE_WALK_AHEAD_VERIFY=1  with it: each answer from a kept run is walked again
                                           the old way; the walked answer is used, a difference
                                           counts as "wrong" (expect 0); the first 16 per thread
                                           since boot are printed (a total that never resets).
                                           Because the walked answer is used, a verify run cannot
                                           judge the picture: do the picture check with =1 alone.
Lines (stderr): at the first page table change or question,
  "EDEN_NX2_PAGE_RANGE_WALK_AHEAD=1: unbroken GPU memory runs are walked up to 256 MiB ahead ...";
  per asking thread, at most every 10 s (checked once per 65,536 questions),
  "EDEN_NX2_PAGE_RANGE_WALK_AHEAD 10s (this thread): calls N, kept-run hits H, walks ahead W
  (M MiB walked), runs dropped by a map change D, fell back (map change in flight) F, map changes C,
  address space switches A, verified V, wrong X (since boot T)". Counts are per window (reset at
  each line) except "since boot". "calls" and "fell back" leave out the entry fall-back (map change
  in flight at entry, size 0, address out of range), which returns before counting, so read calls
  as about the bind rate, not the exact one. The first line comes at the 65,536th call on that
  thread, not after 10 s.

Cost when off: the same behaviour, not the same bytes. Per question one guarded load of a
function-local static bool, then the old code; per page table change (a few dozen a second) the
same load, a scope guard and two branches on it. map_serial moves exactly as before. No log, no
lock, no table, no counter. The new members (a mutex, a 1 KiB log, one atomic) sit in each
MemoryManager.

Reader ordering (his design, kept on purpose): there is no acquire fence between the walk and the
mapping_is_current() re-check, so in the C++ model the walk's plain loads may move after it. On the
PS5's x86 with locked writes this does not show in practice, and the old exact table had the same
race with no check at all. Do not use it to explain away a VERIFY "wrong" line.

Risk when on (why the counters): a miss walks up to 256 MiB (65,536 small pages), and the 16 runs
per thread replace our 4096-entry exact table. Where binds sit in many separate runs, misses can
cost more than today: "walks ahead" near "calls" and a large "MiB walked" mean that. A thread that
asks two GPU address spaces in turn empties its runs at each switch ("address space switches").

Checks: git apply --check on a fresh copy of t130/stack/src (memory_manager.cpp offsets 6-24 lines
from his tree); not an anchor file (prepare-vulkan-port.py and headless/CMakeLists.txt do not edit
video_core/memory_manager.*). Host test of his run/log logic against a plain page walk (small
pages, random maps and unmaps, 800,000 questions): 0 wrong.
Review fixes (review, minors 1-5): the verify print limit is a per-thread total
since boot (port addition wrong_total, also shown in the 10 s line), the verify arm's picture
limit, the counter gaps, the ordering note and the off-cost wording. His lines are unchanged.
